    <!-- ══ BENEFIT ROWS: UV + ENERGY ══ -->
    <section class="of-benefits">
      <div class="of-benefits__container">
        <div class="of-benefits__header">
          <div class="of-benefits__eyebrow">Collection Protection</div>
          <h2 class="of-benefits__headline">How Window Film Solves What Costly Glass Replacements Cannot</h2>
          <p class="of-benefits__intro">Museum window film for Philadelphia institutions and library window film for Philadelphia branches solve preservation, visitor comfort, energy, and security problems inside the existing glass.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/mu-uv.jpg" alt="UV conservation window film protecting artwork and artifacts in a Philadelphia museum gallery" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Preservation</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Block 99% of UV Radiation to Meet AIC Conservation Standards</h3><p class="of-row__body">UV radiation is the primary cause of irreversible fading in museum collections. The American Institute for Conservation recommends UV exposure below 75 lux and UV radiation below 10 uW/lm for sensitive artifacts, textiles, paper, photographs, and organic materials.</p><p class="of-row__body">Window film achieves 99% UV rejection, making it the most cost-effective path to AIC-referenced compliance without replacing windows. Learn more about <a href="/benefits/uv-blocking-window-film-philadelphia/">UV-blocking window film in Philadelphia</a> and review conservation guidance from the <a href="https://www.culturalheritage.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Institute for Conservation</a>.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Protects pigments, textiles, paper, photographs, and organic materials.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Clear films preserve visible appearance while blocking damaging ultraviolet radiation.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Documentation supports collection care, facilities, and preservation review.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/mu-glare.jpg" alt="Glare reduction window film improving artwork viewing and reading comfort in Philadelphia cultural institution" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Visitor Experience</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Eliminate Glare Without Losing the Natural Light That Makes Collections Come Alive</h3><p class="of-row__body">Glare from uncontrolled sunlight washes out artwork, exhibit labels, display screens, and reading tables. In public libraries, afternoon light can make study areas unusable just when patron demand is high.</p><p class="of-row__body"><a href="/benefits/glare-reduction/">Glare reduction film</a> cuts visible glare by 70-90% while maintaining the color quality visitors need to appreciate collections. Museum directors report measurable improvement in dwell time when gallery glare is resolved; the room stays daylit, just without the harshness.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Improves artwork viewing, label reading, research tables, and public computer areas.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>No blackout effect; natural light remains part of the visitor experience.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Useful in galleries, reading rooms, classrooms, lobbies, and exhibit halls.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/mu-energy.jpg" alt="Solar control film helping Philadelphia museum HVAC maintain collection climate conditions" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Energy Management</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Reduce the HVAC Loads That Strain Collection Climate Control Systems</h3><p class="of-row__body">Museums and libraries run HVAC continuously to maintain collection-safe humidity and temperature ranges, often 68-72°F and 45-55% RH. Solar heat gain through large glass facades directly stresses those systems and makes localized hot zones harder to control.</p><p class="of-row__body">Solar control film rejects up to 86% of solar heat gain before it enters the building, reducing HVAC load and energy costs. Many institutions see payback within 3-5 years; review <a href="/benefits/energy-savings/">energy-saving window film</a> options and <a href="https://www.imls.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IMLS guidance on sustainable library facilities</a>.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Supports temperature and humidity stability near glazed facades.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Reduces solar heat at the glass before it becomes an HVAC load.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Useful for large glazed lobbies, reading rooms, atriums, and gallery-adjacent windows.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/mu-security.jpg" alt="Security window film protecting Philadelphia museum entry glass and display areas" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Security</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Security Film That Protects Collections and Visitors at Every Entry Point</h3><p class="of-row__body">Museums and libraries face smash-and-grab theft, vandalism, and forced entry risks, especially at street-level entry glass, ground-floor windows, and glass-enclosed special collections. Security film holds shattered glass together, delaying entry and reducing flying shards.</p><p class="of-row__body">For display cases, staff areas, and public-facing glass, <a href="/benefits/safety-and-security/">safety and security window film</a> provides an additional protective layer while maintaining visibility. Film can be specified alongside UV conservation goals for a single coordinated glass protection package.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Helps delay forced entry at doors, storefront glass, and vulnerable windows.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Reduces injury risk from flying glass fragments in public areas.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Coordinates with UV, solar, and privacy requirements for institution-wide specifications.</li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ FILM TYPES SLIDER ══ -->
    <section class="of-ftype" id="ofFilmTypes">
      <div class="of-ftype__track" id="ofFtypeTrack">
        <div class="of-ftype__slide of-ftype-active" data-slide="0"><img src="/images/mu-slider-uv.jpg" alt="UV conservation film in a Philadelphia museum gallery" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">01</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">UV Conservation Film.
AIC-Referenced Protection.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Optically clear UV conservation film blocks 99% of ultraviolet radiation for permanent collections, rare books, archives, textiles, photographs, and organic materials.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What UV protection level do AIC standards require?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>AIC recommends UV radiation below 10 uW/lm; our conservation-grade film achieves 99% UV rejection, well within AIC guidelines for permanent collections.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does UV film affect how artwork looks?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>No. Conservation-grade UV film is optically clear and does not change the color rendering index, so artwork appears as the artist intended.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is UV film reversible if standards change?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Window film can be removed without damaging compatible glass, allowing future upgrades to higher-performance film if conservation requirements change.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Review UV conservation film →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="1"><img src="/images/mu-slider-glare.jpg" alt="Glare reduction film improving museum visitor experience" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">02</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Glare Reduction Film.
Clear Views, Comfortable Rooms.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Glare reduction film preserves daylight while reducing harsh brightness that washes out artwork, exhibit labels, reading rooms, and public computer areas.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does glare film improve visitor experience?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>It removes the intense reflections and bright patches that make visitors squint, improving comfort in galleries, lobbies, exhibit halls, and library reading spaces.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will it change color accuracy?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>We specify high-quality films with neutral appearance and appropriate VLT so collections remain visually accurate under daylight and gallery lighting.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it help reading comfort?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Reading room glare reduction helps patrons use tables, screens, periodicals, and study areas without closing blinds or losing the room's natural light.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Solve gallery glare →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="2"><img src="/images/mu-slider-energy.jpg" alt="Solar control and energy window film for museum climate control" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">03</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Solar Control &amp; Energy Film.
Less Load on Climate Systems.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Solar control film rejects heat before it enters glazed museum and library spaces, supporting collection climate management and reducing cooling demand.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How much can HVAC load drop?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Results vary by glass, exposure, and film, but large glazed institutions commonly use solar control film to reduce cooling load and improve comfort near windows.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it help collection climate control?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. By limiting solar heat gain at the glass, film helps HVAC systems maintain temperature and humidity ranges for galleries, archives, and special collections.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What affects energy payback?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Glass area, orientation, current HVAC load, utility rates, and selected film performance all influence payback. We provide documented film data for facilities review.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Compare solar control specs →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="3"><img src="/images/mu-slider-security.jpg" alt="Security and anti-intrusion film on museum entry glass" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">04</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Security &amp; Anti-Intrusion Film.
Delay Entry, Hold Glass.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Security film reinforces vulnerable glass by holding shattered panes together after impact, helping protect collections, visitors, staff, and public-facing entries.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it stop smash-and-grab theft?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Security film does not make glass unbreakable, but it holds broken glass together and forces sustained effort, delaying opportunistic smash-and-grab entry.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does it delay forced entry?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>The film bonds to the glass surface, keeping fragments attached after impact so the opening remains harder to clear and alarms or staff response have more time.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What certifications are available?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Security film systems can be specified with ANSI Z97.1 and related impact documentation, depending on the product, glass, attachment method, and application.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Assess security film →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="4"><img src="/images/mu-slider-privacy.jpg" alt="Privacy film for library special collections and staff-only areas" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">05</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Privacy Film.
Control Sensitive Views.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Frosted, gradient, and one-way privacy films protect special collections areas, staff-only rooms, offices, research spaces, and back-of-house glass.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can privacy film protect special collections?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Frosted and privacy films reduce direct sightlines into restricted reading rooms, conservation labs, storage areas, and special collections offices.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is it useful for staff-only areas?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Staff offices, processing rooms, security desks, and administrative areas can gain privacy while keeping borrowed light through interior glass.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What frosted options are available?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Options include full frost, bands, gradients, etched-glass effects, and custom-cut graphics that coordinate with institutional branding or wayfinding.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Plan privacy film →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5"><img src="/images/mu-slider-decorative.jpg" alt="Decorative film for museum exhibit transitions and donor recognition glass" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">06</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Decorative Film.
Exhibits, Donors, Wayfinding.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Decorative film turns glass into an exhibit, donor, or wayfinding surface without permanent etched glass or full replacement.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can film support exhibit transitions?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Decorative film can create visual transitions between exhibits, manage sightlines, and add removable graphics for temporary or rotating installations.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can donor recognition go on glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Frosted, cut, or printed film can create refined donor recognition glass that is easier to update than etched or replaced panels.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can it match institutional branding?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. We can coordinate frosted partitions, patterns, logos, and wayfinding elements with your brand standards and interior design team.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Design decorative glass →</a></div></div>
      </div>

